{"parentUuid":"6ddbe7e5-2002-467b-b3bd-32250065b2e2","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6f37bbaf-3b2a-4889-9da8-014105b65b82","agentId":"a89d19953587dd79f","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017Eo3cDZmXCKS8YgJ3QB4kN","type":"tool_result","content":"# Metric Definitions and Scoring\n\n## Time to Optimal (MinTimeToOptimal)\n**Measurement:** Elapsed time between `datetime.now()` calls before and after `solver.solve()`.\n\n**Return value:** Actual elapsed seconds if status equals `OPTIMAL`; otherwise `max_time_in_seconds * par_multiplier` (typically 10x penalty).\n\n**Solver invocation:** Sets `random_seed` and `max_time_in_seconds`, optionally configures `relative_gap_limit` and `absolute_gap_limit` if provided.\n\n## Quality Within Timelimit (MinGapWithinTimelimit)\n**Measurement:** Relative gap calculated as `abs(objective_value - best_bound) / max(1, abs(objective_value))`.\n\n**Return value:** The lesser of computed gap or a configured `limit` threshold. Returns `limit` if no feasible solution found.\n\n**Solver invocation:** Sets `random_seed` and `max_time_in_seconds`, then calls `solver.solve()`.\n\n## Gap Integral (MinGapIntegralWithinTimelimit)\n**Measurement:** Extracts `gap_integral` from `solver.response_proto`.\n\n**Return value:** Minimum of configured `limit` or actual gap integral. Returns `limit` if no solution found.\n\n**Solver invocation:** Identical setup—`random_seed` and `max_time_in_seconds` configured before `solver.solve()`.\n\n## Randomness Injection\nEach metric generates a fresh random seed via `random.randint(0, 2**31 - 1)` before every solve invocation, ensuring non-deterministic behavior across repeated runs."}]},"uuid":"ffc4e3b6-7052-4afc-995c-c0ddbce34c49","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:00:26.890Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6ddbe7e5-2002-467b-b3bd-32250065b2e2","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"93bbaf9b-ca0e-42ad-86b3-2274d066afb3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6f37bbaf-3b2a-4889-9da8-014105b65b82","agentId":"a89d19953587dd79f","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K4XPhJ8s6S2F6YCAeayPmf","type":"tool_result","content":"# Parameter Evaluator Post-Optuna Analysis\n\n## Statistical Testing\nThe code does **not** employ formal statistical tests like Mann-Whitney or t-tests. Instead, it uses a simpler threshold-based comparison approach through the `metric.comp()` method, which returns `Comparison` enums (BETTER, EQUAL, WORSE) rather than p-values.\n\n## Parameter Importance Reporting\nYes, the evaluator reports parameter significance. It calculates relative importance by:\n1. Computing the performance difference when each parameter is excluded\n2. Normalizing these differences: `\"diff / total_diff for key, diff in diffs.items()\"`\n3. Storing results in a `contribution` dictionary representing each parameter's proportional impact\n\n## Significance Filtering\nThe filtering mechanism uses a tolerance threshold rather than statistical significance:\n\n- Establishes an `\"accept_as_equal = (self.metric.worst(optuna_baseline) + optuna_baseline.mean()) / 2\"` midpoint\n- Compares each parameter's exclusion score against this threshold\n- Retains parameters where removal causes performance degradation beyond this tolerance\n- Drops parameters that can be excluded without substantial penalty\n\nIf final optimized parameters underperform, the evaluator reverts to the original Optuna parameters and clears the significance dictionary, indicating no reliable filtering occurred."}]},"uuid":"75093525-7b51-4011-b031-d8103e436df2","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:00:28.122Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"93bbaf9b-ca0e-42ad-86b3-2274d066afb3","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"921f1a0c-201d-478f-ae5c-0c904c7fddaa","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6f37bbaf-3b2a-4889-9da8-014105b65b82","agentId":"a89d19953587dd79f","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0111xvczHZr3KSrQz5fE3Hn6","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"cpsat-autotune speedup benchmark example.ipynb medium_hg results\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-autotune: WIP: Tune the hyperparameters of Google's OR-Tools' CP-SAT solver for specific models · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-autotune\"},{\"title\":\"cpsat-autotune · PyPI\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/cpsat-autotune/0.2.0/\"},{\"title\":\"cutlass/examples/python/CuTeDSL/notebooks/benchmark_autotune.ipynb at main · NVIDIA/cutlass\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/NVIDIA/cutlass/blob/main/examples/python/CuTeDSL/notebooks/benchmark_autotune.ipynb\"},{\"title\":\"Support autotune performance metrics during benchmarking · Issue #1480 · pytorch/helion\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/pytorch/helion/issues/1480\"},{\"title\":\"Performance Tuning Guide — PyTorch Tutorials 2.12.0+cu130 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/recipes/recipes/tuning_guide.html\"},{\"title\":\"How I Achieved 2.9× PyTorch Training Speedup with One Line of Code | by chinmay shrivastava | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@cshrivastava2000/how-i-achieved-2-9-pytorch-training-speedup-with-one-line-of-code-a8b8f88d388f\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - JonSnow1807/pytorch-autotune: 🚀 2-4x faster PyTorch training with one line of code. Beats torch.compile by 79%. Zero config, automatic hardware optimization for T4/V100/A100/H100 GPUs.\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/JonSnow1807/pytorch-autotune\"},{\"title\":\"cpsat-autotune\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/cpsat-autotune/\"},{\"title\":\"kernl/experimental/whisper/speedup.ipynb at main · ELS-RD/kernl\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ELS-RD/kernl/blob/main/experimental/whisper/speedup.ipynb\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - meghaagr13/Autotuning-PIO\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/meghaagr13/Autotuning-PIO\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about cpsat-autotune and the medium_hg model:\n\nThe cpsat-autotune project includes an example that loads the medium_hg.pb model file and uses the tune_time_to_optimal function to tune the model with parameters such as max_time_in_seconds=3, n_samples_for_trial=5, n_samples_for_verification=20, and n_trials=50.\n\ncpsat-autotune is a Python library designed to optimize the hyperparameters of Google's OR-Tools CP-SAT solver for specific problem instances. While CP-SAT is already highly optimized for a broad range of generic problems, fine-tuning its parameters for particular problem sets can yield significant performance gains. This tool leverages the optuna optimization library to systematically explore and suggest optimal hyperparameter configurations.\n\nBy tuning the solver's hyperparameters to optimize performance on typical instances, cpsat-autotune can reduce the average time per iteration. This is particularly valuable in ALNS, where a large number of solver calls are made.\n\nThe search results reference the GitHub repository (d-krupke/cpsat-autotune) and PyPI page, which contain the example code using the medium_hg model, though specific numerical speedup results for that particular benchmark were not detailed in the search results.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"a5c89e59-a6ec-4c37-9d7a-86c670e660ff","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:00:31.173Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"921f1a0c-201d-478f-ae5c-0c904c7fddaa","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
